<span style="color:#f00">**Claim 2.**</span>  $K\in (ABC)$ .
*Proof.* It is easy to see that  $K,X,Y,C$  are concylic because  $\angle{XKY}=\angle{XCY}=90^{\circ}$ .
From the first claim,  $\overline{OX}$  is an angle bisector of  $\angle{BXC}$ . So, we can redefine the external angle bisector of  $\angle{BXC}$  to be a line perpendicular to  $\overline{KX}$ . Which means  ${TY}\perp AB$ . The angle chasing from  $\angle{A}$  gives:  $$ \angle{BAC}=90^{\circ}-AYT=\angle{YKC}. $$  Since,  $B,K$  and  $Y$  are collinear. Therefore,  $K\in(ABC)$ .
[rule]
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim 3.**</span>  $T,K$  and  $C$  are collinear.
*Proof.* Observe that  $BKXT$  and  $ACXT$  are cyclic quadrilateral. Perform an angle chasing:  $$ \angle{TKX}=\angle{TBX}=\angle{BXO}=90^{\circ}-\angle{BAC}\implies \angle{BKC}=90^{\circ}+TKX $$  Therefore,  $T,K$  and  $C$  are collinear.
[rule]
Since  $X\in (BKT),(KCY)$ , and  $T=KC\cap AB, Y=BK\cap AC$ ,  $X$  is a miquel point of  $ABKC$ . It is well-known that  $O,P$  and  $X$  are collinear. In conclusion,  $AK,XO$  and  $BC$  are concurrent.

We claim the only solutions are \[(x, y, p) = \{(4, 5, 7), (5, 4, 7), (2, 7, 13), (7, 2, 13), (1, 8, 19), (8, 1, 19)\}\] all of which clearly work. 

Because  $p \mid x^3 + y^3$ , we know  $p \mid x$  if and only if  $p \mid y$ . Now, suppose  $p \mid x, y$  or  $x = pa$  and  $y = pb$  for some  $a, b \in \mathbb{Z^+}$ . This yields \[v_p(x^3 + y^3) = v_p(p^3(a^3 + b^3)) = 3 + v_p(a^3 + b^3) \ge 3\] and \[v_p(p(xy+p)) = 1 + v_p(p^2ab + p) = 2 + v_p(pab + 1) = 2\] which is absurd. Hence, we must have  $p \nmid x, y$ , which also yields \[v_p(p(xy+p)) = 1 + v_p(xy+p) = 1.\] Now, since  $x^3 + y^3 = (x+y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)$ , we have two cases.

Suppose  $p \mid x+y$  or  $x+y = kp$  where  $k \in \mathbb{Z^+}$ . Then, \[p(xy+p) = (x+y)(x^2-xy+y^2) = kp((x-y)^2 + xy)\] \[xy+p = k((x-y)^2 + xy)\] \[xy(k-1) = p - k(x-y)^2.\] Now, if  $k \ge 2$ , we have \[xy(k-1) \ge xy \ge 1 \cdot (kp - 1) > p \ge p - k(x-y)^2.\] Hence, we must have  $k = 1$ , implying  $x+y = p$  and \[x^2 - xy + y^2 = xy + p \implies (x-y)^2 = p.\] This gives \[(x, y) = \left\{ \left( \frac{p + \sqrt{p}}{2}, \frac{p - \sqrt{p}}{2} \right), \left( \frac{p - \sqrt{p}}{2}, \frac{p + \sqrt{p}}{2} \right) \right\} \] meaning there are no  $x, y \in \mathbb{Z^+}$  for this case.

Now, suppose  $p \mid x^2 - xy + y^2$  or  $x^2 - xy + y^2 = kp$  where  $k \in \mathbb{Z^+}$ . These conditions give  $3xy = (x+y)^2 - kp$  and 
\begin{align}
p(xy+p) = (x+y)(x^2-xy+y^2) = kp(x+y) 
\nonumber 3k(x+y) = 3(xy + p) = ((x+y)^2 - kp) + 3p 
p(k-3) = (x+y)(x+y - 3k).
\end{align}
Now, recall that \[v_p((x+y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)) = v_p(p(xy+p)) = 1 \] so  $p \nmid x+y$ . This implies  $p \mid x+y - 3k$  or  $x+y - 3k = mp$  for some  $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ , which simplifies  $(2)$  to  $k = m(x+y) + 3$ . Because  $k \in \mathbb{Z^+}$  and  $x+y \ge 2$ , we know the only possibilities are  $(x, y, m) = (1, 1, -1)$  or  $m \ge 0$ . For the former case, noting  $k = 1$  and using  $x^2 - xy + y^2 = kp$  yields  $p = 1$ , which is invalid. Moreover, if  $m \ge 1$ , then
\begin{align}
\nonumber x+y - mp = 3k = 3m(x+y) + 9 \hspace{52.5mm} \text{(3)}
\end{align}
gives \[(x+y)(1 - 3m) = mp + 9 > 0\] which is a contradiction, so the only remaining possibility is  $m = 0$ . Indeed,  $m = 0$  and  $(3)$  immediately imply  $k = 3$  and  $x+y = 9$ , and using  $(1)$  gives \[xy + p = k(x+y) = 27.\] Thus, testing all possible pairs  $(x, y)$  yields the  $6$  aforementioned solutions, which finishes.  $\blacksquare$

solution_md:
The smallest possible degree is  $2n$ . In what follows, we will frequently write  $A_i = (x_i, y_i)$ , and abbreviate  $f(x_1, y_1, \dots, x_{2n}, y_{2n})$  to  $f(A_1, \dots, A_{2n})$  or as a function of any  $2n$  points.
-----**Bound** Suppose that  $f$  is valid. First, we note a key property:

*Claim.* [Sign of  $f$ ] Either  $f \ge 0$  or  $f \le 0$ .
*Proof.* This follows from the fact that the zero-set of  $f$  is very sparse: if  $f$  takes on a positive and a negative value, we can move  $A_1,\ \dots,\ A_{2n}$  from the negative value to the positive value without ever having them form a regular  $2n$ -gon—a contradiction.  $\square$ The strategy for showing  $\deg f \ge 2n$  is the following. We will animate the points  $A_1,\ \dots,\ A_{2n}$  linearly in a variable  $t$ ; then  $g(t) \stackrel{\text{def}}= f(A_1, \dots, A_{2n})$  will have degree at most  $\deg f$  (assuming it is not zero). The previous lemma establishes that any root of  $g$  must be a multiple root, so if we can show that there are at least  $n$  roots, we will have shown  $\deg g \ge 2n$ , and so  $\deg f \ge 2n$ .

Geometrically, our goal is to exhibit  $2n$  linearly moving points so that they form a regular  $2n$ -gon a total of  $n$  times, but not always form one.

We will do this as follows. Draw  $n$  *mirrors* through the origin, as lines making angles of  $\tfrac{\pi}n$  with each other. Then, any point  $P$  has a total of  $2n$  reflections in the mirrors, as shown below for  $n = 5$ . (Some of these reflections may overlap.)
[asy]
unitsize(60);
int n = 5;
pair P = 0.8 * dir(24);
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
  real ang = i * 180/n;
  draw(dir(ang)--dir(ang+180), arrow=Arrows);
}

pair Q = reflect((1, 0), (-1, 0)) * P;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
  dot(rotate(360*i/n)*(P^^Q), red);
}
[/asy]
Draw the  $n$  angle bisectors of adjacent mirrors. Observe that  $P$ 's reflections form a regular  $2n$ -gon if and only if  $P$  lies on one of the bisectors.
[asy]
unitsize(60);
int n = 5;
pair P = 0.6 * dir(18);
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
real ang = i * 180/n;
draw(dir(ang)--dir(ang+180), arrow=Arrows);
}
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
real ang = (i+1/2) * 180/n;
draw(scale(1.0) * (dir(ang)--dir(ang+180)), dashed+gray(0.5));
}

pair Q = reflect((1, 0), (-1, 0)) * P;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
dot(rotate(360*i/n)*(P^^Q), red);
}
[/asy]
We will animate  $P$  on any line which intersects all  $n$  bisectors, and let  $P_1,\ \dots,\ P_{2n}$  be its reflections. Clearly, these are also all linearly animated, and because of the reasons above, they will form a regular  $2n$ -gon at least  $n$  times. It's not difficult to see that they will not always form a regular  $2n$ -gon, so this establishes  $\deg f \ge 2n$  for the reasons described previously.
-----**Construction** We will construct a suitable  $f$  as a sum of squares. This means that, if we write  $f = f_1^2 + \dots + f_k^2$ , then  $f = 0$  if and only if  $f_1 = \dots = f_k = 0$ , and that if their degrees are  $d_1,\ \dots,\ d_k$ , then  $f$  has degree at most  $2 \max(d_1, \dots, d_k)$ .

Thus, it is sufficient to exhibit several polynomials, all of degree at most  $n$ , such that  $2n$  points are the vertices of a regular  $2n$ -gon if and only if the polynomials are all zero at those points.

First of all, we will instead find a polynomial which has this property, but only when points with sum zero are input. This still solves the problem, because then we can choose
\[ f(A_1 - \overline{A}, \dots, A_{2n} - \overline{A}), \]
where  $\overline{A}$  is the centroid of  $A_1,\ \dots,\ A_{2n}$ . This has the upshot that we can now always assume  $A_1 + \dots + A_{2n} = 0$ , which will simplify the ensuing discussion.

First, we will impose the constraints that all  $|A_i|^2 = x_i^2 + y_i^2$  are equal. This uses multiple degree  $2$  constraints.

Now, we may assume that the points  $A_1,\ \dots,\ A_{2n}$  lie on a circle with center  $0$ , and  $A_1 + \dots + A_{2n} = 0$ . If this circle has radius  $0$ , then all  $A_i$  coincide, and we may ignore this case.

Otherwise, the circle has positive radius. We will use the following lemma.

*Lemma.* Suppose that  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  are complex numbers of the same nonzero magnitude, and suppose that  $a_1^k + \dots + a_{2n}^k = 0$  for all  $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ . Then  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  form a regular  $2n$ -gon centered at the origin. (Conversely, this is easily seen to be sufficient.)

*Proof.* Since all the hypotheses are homogenous, we may assume (mostly for convenience) that  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  lie on the unit circle. By Newton's sums, we obtain that the  $k^{\text{th}}$  symmetric sums of  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  are all zero for  $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ .

Taking conjugates gives that we also have
\[ a_1^{-k} + \dots + a_{2n}^{-k} = 0 \]
for all  $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ .

Thus, we can repeat the above logic to obtain that the  $k^{\text{th}}$  symmetric sums of  $a_1^{-1},\ \dots,\ a_{2n}^{-1}$  are also all zero for  $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ . However, these are simply the  $(2n-k)^{\text{th}}$  symmetric sums of  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  (divided by  $a_1 \cdots a_{2n}$ ), so the first  $2n-1$  symmetric sums of  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  are all zero. This implies that  $a_1,\ \dots,\ a_{2n}$  form a regular  $2n$ -gon centered at the origin.  $\square$ We will encode all of these constraints into our polynomial. More explicitly, write  $a_r = x_r + y_ri$ ; then the constraint  $a_1^k + \dots + a_{2n}^k = 0$  can be expressed as  $p_k + q_k i = 0$ , where  $p_k$  and  $q_k$  are real polynomials in the coordinates. To incorporate this, simply impose the constraints  $p_k = 0$  and  $q_k = 0$ ; these are conditions of degree  $k \le n$ , which is okay.

To recap, taking the sum of squares of all of these constraints gives a polynomial  $f$  of degree at most  $2n$  which works whenever  $A_1 + \dots + A_{2n} = 0$ . Finally, the centroid-shifting trick gives a polynomial which works in general, as wanted.
-----**Remark.** [Odd number of points] It is natural to wonder about what happens when the number of points is an odd integer  $n \ge 3$ . There is a construction of degree  $\max(4, n-1)$ , which is similar to the construction described for  $n$  even.

To obtain a bound, we could try to proceed as we did earlier, by solving the following:
<blockquote>
There are  $n$  linearly animated points on the plane. Suppose that they form the vertices of a regular  $n$ -gon exactly  $N$  times, where  $N$  is finite. What is the maximum possible value of  $N$ ?
</blockquote>
This problem is actually interesting in its own right (try it!): the answer turns out to be  $\max(2, d)$ , where  $d$  is the largest proper divisor of  $n$ . (Observe that this gives  $\tfrac12n$  for  $n$  even, as expected.) So, this gives a lower bound of  $\max(4, 2d)$  on the degree.

Unfortunately, if  $n$  is odd, this is only sharp when  $n = 3$  and  $n = 5$ . My guess is that the true answer is closer to  $n-1$  then  $\max(4, 2d)$ , but I don't see any way to improve either side.
-----
Here's some commentary and some info about the development timeline of the problem, if you're curious. I've also attached my original proposal.

<details><summary>Commentary and developer notes (warning: really long)</summary>At some point, I had learned that the regular pentagon is the unique <details><summary>two-distance set</summary>meaning that there are (at most) two different pairwise distances between the points</details> in the plane consisting of  $5$  points. For some reason, this got me thinking: this fact gives rise to a polynomial for detecting whether  $5$  points form a regular pentagon. In particular, out of any three distances, two must be equal. Using this fact, we can easily cosntruct a degree- $12$  detector for regular pentagons. I thought this was neat, but didn't have any idea of what to do with this fact, so I shelved it.

<details><summary>Well, actually...</summary>The degree- $12$  polynomial mentioned doesn't quite work, if you allow some but not all of the points to be equal. For example, choosing one point and four copies of another point breaks it, because that is still a two-distance multiset. At this stage in development, though, I was only allowing five pairwise different points to be input into the polynomial, so this wasn't an issue.

For what it's worth, you can still make a degree- $12$  detector in this spirit which works if some points can be equal: instead, assert that for any two distances, either they're equal, or one is  $\tfrac{1+\sqrt5}2$  times the other.</details>

(Aside: Some of you might remember the [ill-fated USA proposal to RMM 2020](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h2694136p23396136), which looks similar to this problem. I don't remember the timeline fully, so I don't remember whether I was inspired by that problem or not. Regardless, considering how long the development timeline of this RMM 2023 problem is, it's quite likely that I was at least subconsciously influenced by it.)

At various points, I would dabble with this regular pentagon detection idea, but never really have any insights on a construction beating degree  $12$ , or a lower bound greater than  $4$  (the polynomial can't take on values of both signs, and there's a linear animation which forms a regular pentagon twice: just fix three points and swap the other two).

After a while, I reasoned that the most efficient polynomials would probably rely on global ideas, rather than local ones, as I had been trying. With globality in mind, I naturally started thinking about roots of unity and complex numbers on the unit circle. I knew that the first  $4$  power sums being zero gives that they're spaced apart  $72$  degrees: this gives a polynomial of degree  $8$ , beating the previous record of  $12$ .

Fortunately for me, I also knew that you didn't actually need all  $4$  power sums to conclude that the points are equally spaced: the lowest  $2$  are enough. (If I hadn't known this fact, I doubt this problem would've ever become a reality.) With this, we can get a polynomial of degree  $4$ , settling the problem.

I thought this was a pretty funny concept for a problem, and prepared a write-up of the solution. Overall, I thought the problem was mostly cursed than anything else, so naturally I wanted to send it to USEMO if possible. (Sorry, Evan.)

However, with the USEMO deadline edging closer, I came back to the problem. *Surely this isn't how this problem is going end up, right?* I mean, it's just so... specific (only pentagons?) and off-putting. Surely there's something bigger out there. Maybe it's too difficult for me to find, but I have to genuinely try.

With that, I went back to the drawing board. With  $5$ -gons settled, and the solution working for  $3$ -gons as well, the natural target was  $(2n+1)$ -gons, for which this yields a polynomial of degree  $\max(4, 2n)$ .

But how would I get a lower bound greater than  $4$ ? The only idea I had to prove any lower bound was to linearly animate the points, and hope that they form a regular polygon often (but not always). However, try as I might, I couldn't make them form a regular polygon more than twice.

Defeated, I decided to stop artifically restricting myself, and looked at the case of  $2n$ -gons, for which the construction idea above yields a polynomial of degree  $2n$ . I had liked the odd case better since odd polygons just seemed like more natural targets (especially with a local approach) and the construction was slightly more efficient for odd polygons, but I decided to expand my horizons. (Considering what the final problem is, this is pretty funny in hindsight.)

With no ideas to improve the construction, I went back to trying to prove bounds greater than  $4$ , but for even polygons. After a while of no results, I decided to settle down and demonstrate that this really can't be done: it's not possible for the points to form a regular polygon more than twice (without them always forming one).

You might be able to tell where this is going. After following this train of thought for a while, I got that if the number of points is prime, then the points can form a regular polygon at most twice. But what I didn't expect to find was that for  $6$  points, it just *might* be possible for them to form a regular polygon  $3$  times.

[hide = 'Details—essentially, the solution to the "actually interesting in its own right" problem above']
Suppose that the points formed a regular polygon at least three times. Change the points' velocities by the same amount so that their centroid  $O$  remains constant. Since regular polygons have centroid and circumcenter coinciding, this means that the points are all equidistant from  $O$  at least three times.

However, each point's squared distance from  $O$  is quadratic in time, so this means that the points are always all equidistant from  $O$ . Moreover, the lines that the points travel on must be equally distant from  $O$ . (Why? Each point has a unique time when it's closest to  $O$ . By equidistance, these times must all be the same, and then the common distance is the distance from  $O$  to these lines.)

OK, this is interesting. Each point's velocity can now be characterized with a single unit vector, denoting its direction of travel. (It's not difficult to see that the points must have the same speed.)

However, there are two directions for points to be traveling: essentially, clockwise around  $O$ , or counterclockwise. Then, the figures formed by the clockwise points as time varies are spirally similar with center  $O$ , and similarly for the counterclockwise points. So, times when the points comprise a regular polygon essentially correspond to ways to fit these two figures to form a regular polygon. (This wasn't the way I originally thought about it, but it's much easier to understand than my original idea.)

At this point, it's not difficult to work out that for  $n$  points, this can happen at most  $d$  times, where  $d$  is the largest proper divisor of  $n$ . (This is starting to sound familiar...) To see this, note that the number ways to fit the two figures together is bounded by the number of rotational symmetries of either figure. Each rotation preserving the figure must be a multiple of the smallest one, and for neither figure to be empty, we can't have all  $n$  rotations, so the next-best possible is  $d$ .</details>

I didn't have a construction, but there were hints that it was possible, and I just needed to figure out how to make it work. The proof above suggests that to get  $3$  instances of regularity, I should have three points form a clockwise equilateral triangle, and three points forming a counterclockwise one.

And after a while... this happened.
[asy]
int n = 3; int m = 2*n;
real u = 1.2, v = 0.15;
pen[] cols = {heavycyan, heavymagenta};
transform t = rotate(90);
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
pair A = dir((2*i)*360/m), B = dir((2*i+2)*360/m);
draw(t * (A--B), cols[0]);
dot(t * foot(0, A, B), cols[0]);
draw(t * scale(u) * ((((A+B)/2)+v*(B-A)/2) -- (((A+B)/2)+v*(A-B)/2)), cols[0], EndArrow);
}
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
pair A = dir((2*i+1)*360/m), B = dir((2*i+3)*360/m);
draw(t * (A--B), cols[1]);
dot(t * foot(0, A, B), cols[1]);
draw(t * scale(u) * ((((A+B)/2)-v*(B-A)/2) -- (((A+B)/2)-v*(A-B)/2)), cols[1], EndArrow);
}
for (int i = 0; i < 2*n; ++i)
dot(t * dir(i*360/m), orange);
[/asy]
Imagine the cyan points move clockwise around the cyan triangle, and the magenta points move counterclockwise around the magenta triangle, as shown. Then we have  $3$  instances of regularity: the one shown in the image, and two instances where the points coincide with the orange points.

With this, we obtain a lower bound of  $6$  for the  $6$ -point case, and that resolved the  $6$ -point case fully: finally, a case other than  $3$  and  $5$  (and  $4$  too, I guess) was resolved, and perhaps more importantly, the eternal lower bound of  $4$  was finally broken. It can't be overstated how important it was that I was willing to investigate this route of attack so deeply: without doing so, this would've remained a weird pentagon problem until the end of time.

Now that this approach had given concrete results, who knew how far it could be taken? With the victory over the  $6$ -point case, I decided to look at the  $2n$ -point case more, because I needed  $n$  instances of regularity, which the proof above suggests should be possible, and it also suggests how to do it: make half the points form a clockwise  $n$ -gon, and the other half form a counterclockwise  $n$ -gon. With this, eventually I had the idea to use mirrors, which just... solved the entire thing, and quite cleanly too.

I still remember sitting in disbelief that in the span of about an hour (maybe? I don't remember; it's been a while), the problem had gone from being completely impossible to being blown wide open, with the even case being completely resolved. Eventually, I calmed myself down, and wrote an outline of the solution on paper, just to make sure I didn't miss anything. Once I went home, I decided to type up the thing too, just to serve as an additional check (and so I didn't have to type it up later).

The problem was finally done. I could try to solve the odd case as well, but to be honest, I didn't really want to. I was still basking in my win, and didn't need the defeat that the odd case would surely deal me. The problem still feels quite natural being restricted to one parity: in my mind, odd and even regular polygons are qualitatively pretty different, so I was fine with it.

With a finished product (that I was honestly willing to call *good*, albeit still pretty cursed), I faced every composer's nightmare: actually publishing the thing. If getting some of my other problems published was walking up a hill, getting this one published would be walking straight up a cliff with no equipment. Sure, this problem might be good, but it is still cursed, and on top of that, it's *really freaking hard*: maybe too hard for a 4.5 hour exam entirely. (Looking at the results, maybe I wasn't so insane for thinking this.) It requires a lot of technical skill as well as a lot of ingenuity, and I don't know of many other problems that are so demanding on both fronts. (Probably because I don't solve them.)

USEMO is one of the most flexible contests for authors (and the original intended destination, as you might remember), but the deadline for USEMO 2022 had long passed by this point, and so had the deadline for USA TST 2023. Though, I doubt this problem would have much of a chance to get onto USA TST: the reviewers for USA TST are generally sensible, and can tell when a problem is too hard to be a fair problem for team selection. This problem was really going to suffer the same fate as that RMM 2020 proposal, huh? What a sad way to go out.

I needed to find a contest with less sensible reviewers. So, of course, I set my sights on RMM. (For legal purposes: I'm joking. That was a joke.) In fact, RMM had a precedent for not accepting super-hard problems: look at what they did to that RMM 2020 proposal. But I didn't really have any other options, unless I wanted to wait to submit to contests later in 2023, like USA TSTST.

So I sent the problem to RMM 2023, so that once it gets rejected, I could then submit to a contest later in 2023; at least the problem will be doing *something* other than just sitting around and not getting sent anywhere, and as we all know from three years ago, it's not good to just sit at home all day.

Then, several months later, the problem appeared as RMM3. *Oh. Wait, what? They actually chose it? Why?* Sure, it might be good, but it's clearly unsuitable: it's pretty cursed, and if you haven't noticed by now, it's kinda hard. Did they only get one algebra submission? (Clearly not, considering RMM5.) Did Navid Safaei (on the PSC for RMM 2023, and known for many difficult polynomial problems) jump on this problem and force it on the contest, to the chagrin of the other PSC members?

I'll never know. But hey, if any RMM 2023 PSC members are reading this, thanks? By some miracle, I don't need to think about where to send this problem anymore, and I'm glad that the unfortunate RMM 2020 proposal could at least have a similar-looking problem appear on a contest. (I still stand by my belief that the authors of that problem got robbed.)

Eventually, I was able to gather that the problem was much more positively received than I was expecting it to be. One quote I received was "everybody (leaders, coordinators, etc) wanted to know who came up with such a beautiful problem." Interesting choice of adjective for a problem that was still filed as "cursed" in my head, but I'll take it. Though, I imagine the contestants probably had adjectives other than "beautiful" in mind when thinking of this problem. (Sorry... but like, not really though. I hope you understand.)

I was also informed that there were actually other ways of establishing the lower bound, much to my surprise: as said above, the linear animation idea was the only approach I had at all for getting a bound, so being able to see some other approaches (like the circle animation idea mentioned in the official solutions, which I was sure was doomed to fail) was quite nice.

And... yeah, that's about it. I don't really have anything else to say about this problem. Thanks for reading, if you've made it this far, and I hope you enjoyed the problem (with my sympathies, if you competed) as well as this commentary.
[/hide]

