We invert about  $(BC)$ . This sends  $(BME)$  to  $BE$  and  $(CMF)$  to  $CF$ , and it sends their mutual tangents to circles passing through  $M$  that are also tangent to  $BE$  and  $CF$ . The intersection of these two circles by symmetry clearly lies on the reflection  $M'$  of  $M$  over the angle bisector of  $\angle BHC$ . The image of  $(ABC)$  is  $(HBC)$ , and the image of  $AD$ (where  $D$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $BC$ ) is the circle with diameter  $HD'$  where  $D$  is the inverse of  $H$ .

From here on out, we really only work in triangle  $\triangle BHC$  and it is totally ok to delete  $A,E,F$ .

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim.**</span> If  $M'$  lies on  $(BHC)$ , then  $2(BH-CH)^2 = BC^2$ .

Working in triangle  $\triangle BHC$ , we know that the reflection of  $M'$  over the angle bisector lies on the symmedian of  $(BHC)$ , but it also has length  $HM$ , so the symmedian chord has the same length as the median. But we also by similar triangles have \[HM\cdot HM' = HB\cdot HC\text{,}\]
and thus  $HM = \sqrt{BH\cdot CH}$ . Of course, \[HM = \sqrt{\frac{2BH^2 + 2CH^2 - BC^2}4\text{,}}\]
setting these equal of course gives us  $2(BH-CH)^2 = BC^2$ .

If we also want  $\angle MM'D' = 90$ , note that we thus want the midpoint of  $MD'$  to be equidistant from  $M$  and  $M'$ , and thus clearly that the midpoint of  $MD'$  to lie on the angle bisector of  $\angle BHC$ . In other words, if the angle bisector of  $\angle BHC$  intersects  $BC$  at  $L$ , then clearly we want  $2ML = \frac{BM^2}{MD}$ , or  $2ML\cdot MD = BM^2$ . WLOG  $CH > BH$ : then we want \[2\cdot \frac{CH-BH}{2(CH+BH)}\cdot BC \cdot \frac{CH^2-BH^2}{2BC} = \frac14 BC^2\text{,}\] which by difference of squares is equivalent to  $2(CH-BH)^2 = BC^2$ , done.

Complex bash with  $b=-1$ ,  $c=1$ . Let  $(BME)\cap(CMF)=\{M,S\}$ .

An inversion about the unit circle maps  $(BME)$  and  $(CMF)$  to  $BE$  and  $CF$ , respectively, so it maps  $S$  to  $BE\cap CF$ , the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC$ . Thus, it suffices to show that  $k=\frac1s$ .

Now, the circumcenter of  $(BME)$  is given by
\[o_1=\left|\begin{matrix}0&0&1s&s\overline s&1-1&1&1\end{matrix}\right|\div\left|\begin{matrix}0&0&1s&\overline s&1-1&-1&1\end{matrix}\right|=\frac{s(1+\overline s)}{\overline s-s}.\]
Similarly, the circumcenter of  $(CMF)$  is given by
\[o_1=\left|\begin{matrix}0&0&1s&s\overline s&11&1&1\end{matrix}\right|\div\left|\begin{matrix}0&0&1s&\overline s&11&1&1\end{matrix}\right|=\frac{s(-1+\overline s)}{\overline s-s}.\]
Now, the exsimilicenter is given by
\begin{align*}
k&=\frac{|o_2|o_1-|o_1|o_2}{|o_2|-|o_1|}
&=\frac{s(\frac{|o_2|+|o_1|}{|o_2|-|o_1|}+\overline s)}{\overline s-s}
&=\frac{s(\frac{|-1+s|+|1+s|}{|-1+s|-|1+s|}+\overline s)}{\overline s-s}
&=\frac{s(\frac{-1-s\overline s-|s^2-1|}{\overline s+s}+\overline s)}{\overline s-s}
&=\frac{s(\overline s^2-1-|s^2-1|)}{\overline s^2-s^2}.
\end{align*}
To show that  $k=\frac1s$ , it now suffices to show that  $s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2=0$ .
Now, we use the fact that  $K$  lies on  $(ABC)$ .
\begin{align*}
\frac{(k+1)(\overline k-1)}{(k-1)(\overline k+1)}&=\frac{(s+1)(\overline s-1)}{(s-1)(\overline s+1)}
\frac{1-k\overline k}{k-\overline k}&=\frac{1-s\overline s}{s-\overline s}
\frac{\frac{(\overline s^2-s^2)^2+s\overline s(\overline s^2-1-|s^2-1|)(s^2-1-|s^2-1|)}{(\overline s^2-s^2)^2}}{\frac{s(\overline s^2-1-|s^2-1|)+\overline s(s^2-1-|s^2-1|)}{\overline s^2-s^2}}&=\frac{1-s\overline s}{s-\overline s}
(\overline s^2-s^2)^2+s\overline s(2(\overline s^2-1)(s^2-1)-(s^2+\overline s^2-2)|s^2-1|)&=-(1-s\overline s)(s+\overline s)^2(s\overline s-1-|s^2-1|)
s^4\overline s^2+s^2\overline s^4-s^4-s^2\overline s^2-\overline s^4+s^2+\overline s^2&=(2s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2)|s^2-1|
(s^4\overline s^2+s^2\overline s^4-s^4-s^2\overline s^2-\overline s^4+s^2+\overline s^2)^2&=(2s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2)^2(s^2-1)(\overline s^2-1)
(s^2-1)^2(\overline s^2-1)^2(s^2+\overline s^2)^2&=(2s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2)^2(s^2-1)(\overline s^2-1)
(s^2-1)(\overline s^2-1)(s^2-\overline s^2)^2(s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2)&=0
s^2\overline s^2-s^2-\overline s^2&=0
\end{align*}
as desired.

