Inverting around the circle with diameter  $BC$  maps the circumcircle of  $BME$  to  $BE$  and the circumcircle of  $CMF$  to  $CF$ , so the tangents of the two circles map to circles tangent to  $BE$  and  $CF$  and intersect at  $M$ . Therefore,  $K$  maps to the reflection of  $H$  over the angle bisector of  $BHC$ . Since the circumcircle of  $ABC$  maps to the circumcircle of  $BHC$ , we get that  $K$  maps to the intersection of the  $H$ -symmedian of  $BHC$  with the circumcircle of  $BHC$ , which is the reflection of the  $A$ -queue point of  $ABC$  over  $BC$ . Therefore,  $K$  must be the reflection of  $H$  over  $BC$ , so  $AK\perp BC$ .

The following generalization is also true:
If  $Q$  is the  $A$ -queue point,  $X=QK\cap(ABC)$ , and  $Y$  is on  $(ABC)$  satisfying  $XY\parallel KM$ , then  $AH\cap YM$  lies on  $(ABC)$ .

Note that points  $B, C, E, F$  are inscribed inside the circle of diameter  $\overline{BC}$  with center  $M$ . Let  $Q$  be the Miquel point of quadrilateral  $BCEF$ . By the standard facts,  $Q$  lies on  $\odot(BME)$  and  $\odot(CMF)$  and  $\angle MQA = 90^{\circ}$ . Therefore, line  $QM$  meets  $\odot(ABC)$  for a second time at the antipode  $A'$  of  $A$ .


