Suppose Freyja constructs a graph on  $m+n-2$  vertices numbered  $1$  thru  $m+n-2$ , drawing an edge between any two vertices whose labels differ by  $m$  or by  $n$ . Since she can determine the values of  $a_{i+m} - a_i$  and  $a_{i+n} - a_i$  for each  $i$ , each component of the graph represents a set of variables whose pairwise differences are known values.**Claim:** This graph has precisely two components.**Proof:** For any integer  $k$ , consider the vertices with a label congruent to  $k \pmod{n}$ : as long as  $k$  is not  $0$  or  $n-1$ , one of these said vertices will connect to a vertex congruent to  $k+m \pmod{n}$ . Any two vertices congruent mod  $n$  are connected, so the claim follows.

From the claim above, the vertices labelled  $m$  and  $n$  are not connected, so Freyja can parameterize each of  $a_1, \dots, a_{m+n-2}$  as either  $a_m + K$  or  $a_n + K$  for some  $K$ . Since Freyja knows  $a_1 + \dots + a_n$  and  $a_1 + \dots a_m$ , she has a system of equations of the form
