Now we prove that  $(m-1)^2 + (n-1)^2$  guesses are necessary for Freyja. I couldn't figure this part out, but I don't want everything above to go to waste – as such, this is copied from Evan Chen's solution.

As mentioned above, the board is periodic modulo  $mn$ , so we can interpret Tsvety's secret values as an  $(mn)^2$  dimensional vector. Due to the condition of the problem, the set  $S$  of Tsvety's possible vectors is closed under addition.

Consider the set  $A$  of vectors representing a board in which every  $1 \times m$  and  $m \times 1$  strip sum to  $0$ . These vectors all belong to  $S$  (they are valid boards), and these vectors lie in a space with dimension at least  $(m-1)^2$ , since Tsvetsy can arbitrarily assign values to some  $(m-1) \times (m-1)$  square within the  $mn \times mn$  board.

Similarly, the set  $B$  of vectors representing a board in which every  $1 \times n$  and  $n \times 1$  strip sum to  $0$  form a subspace of  $S$  with dimension at least  $(n-1)^2$ . Importantly, sets  $A$  and  $B$  consist of linearly independent vectors, since they only share one common vector, the zero vector. So, spaces  $A$  and  $B$  are orthogonal and thus span a subspace of  $S$  (since  $S$  is closed under addition) of dimension of at least  $(m-1)^2 + (n-1)^2$ .

