The answer is all positive integer  $n$ . Replace "red" and "not red" with  $1$  and  $0$  respectively.**<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>** It is possible for all positive integers  $2^k$  for  $k=0,1,2,\ldots$ .
The motivation: we take  $k+1$  labellings  $\mathcal L_0,\mathcal L_2,\ldots, \mathcal L_k$  where the square  $(x,y)$  has a  $1$  in  $\mathcal L_t$  if and only if  $2^t\mid x$  and  $2^{k-t}\mid y$ . Then, in the final labelling:  $$ L(x,y)=
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{ if }(x,y)\text{ is }1\text{ in an odd number of }\mathcal L_t\text{'s}
0 & \text{ if }(x,y)\text{ is }1\text{ in an even number of }\mathcal L_t\text{'s}
\end{cases} $$  To prove that any rectangle of the form  $2^a\times 2^{k-a}$  for  $a=0,1,\ldots, k$  ( $2^a$  in width,  $2^{k-a}$  in height) has an odd number of  $1$ 's, we will show that  $2^a\times 2^{k-a}$  has an odd number of  $1$ 's in  $\mathcal L_t$  if and only if  $t=a$ . 

If  $t=a$ , the result is evident - there is exactly one  $1$  of  $\mathcal L_a$  in any  $2^a\times 2^{k-a}$  rectangle.

If  $t<a$ , then any row of the  $2^a\times 2^{k-a}$  rectangle containing a  $1$  contains  $\frac{2^a}{2^t}=2^{a-t}$   $1$ 's, which is an even number. Thus, there is an even number of  $1$ 's in the rectangle. The  $t>a$  case can be handled similarly (columns).

In the final labelling  $L$ , for any  $2^a\times 2^{k-a}$  rectangle let  $S_i$  be the sum of entries of the rectangle under labelling  $\mathcal L_i$  for  $i=0,1,\ldots, k$ . Thus, the number of  $1$ 's in the rectangle under  $L$  has same parity as:  $$ S_0+S_1+\cdots + S_k = (S_0+S_1+\cdots+ S_{a-1}+S_{a+1}+\cdots +S_k)+S_a $$  which is odd, as needed. Thus, it is possible for any  $n=2^k$  for integer  $k\geq 0$ .  $\square$ To finish, let  $n=2^kr$  where  $r$  is an odd integer and use the labelling for  $2^k$ . For rectangle with dimensions  $a\times b$  ( $ab=n$ ) take  $c,d$  such that  $2^c\mid a, 2^d\mid b$  and  $c+d=a$ . Then partition the  $a\times b$  rectangle into  $r$  rectangles of size  $c\times d$ : each of these contain an odd number of  $1$ 's, so the total number of  $1$ 's in the  $a\times b$  rectangle is congruent to  $r$  which is odd.

The answer is all  $n$ .

Let  $n=a\cdot 2^b$  where  $a$  is odd. Then the coloring for  $2^b$  will also work here, because a  $x\cdot 2^s$  by  $y\cdot 2^t$  rectangle where  $xy=a,s+t=b$  can be tiled with  $xy$   $2^s$  by  $2^t$  rectangles which each have an odd number of red tiles.

So we may assume  $n$  is a power of  $2$ . We will construct a coloring of a grid where top left is  $(1,1)$  and bottom right is  $(n,n)$  and all rectangles of area  $n$  have an odd number of red tiles. This finishes because we can then just repeat this pattern, and any rectangle that crosses the border can be saved by the things it shares a row/column with that don't cross the border, as well as  $1$  by  $n$  tiles. For example:

1001
1001
0000
0000

This can be saved by adding the first row, second row, and the box consisting of the zeroes on the first two rows. We are always adding an odd number of things because if we only consider the non-stick blocks and only the stick blocks, they cover the same area, so there is an even number in total (counting the one that crosses the border).

Now we claim that greedy just works. Let the "end" of a rectangle inside this  $n$  by  $n$  square be its bottom-right cell. Then, just go in lexicographical order of the cells, arbitrarily choosing whether to color a non-end. Then whenever we reach an end, if it is the end of exactly one rectangle, we may color it accordingly. Otherwise, it is the end of at least two rectangles. Say two of these are  $2^a$  by  $2^b$  and  $2^c$  by  $2^d$ , respectively, and assume WLOG  $a<c$  and  $b>d$ . Then the  $2^c$  by  $2^b$  rectangle that ends at this cell must also be fully inside the big square, so by induction or whatever, if the earlier rectangles lined up then these two will line up too. Thus we will never reach a "disagreement" at any cell.  $\blacksquare$ <details><summary>Code that verifies that this works</summary>[pywindow]
