What is the reasoning for assuming  $a=b$ ?

Let  $f(a,b,c)=ab+bc+ca$ . Let  $g(a,b,c)=abc$ . Let  $h(a,b,c)=a+b+c$ . Maximizing  $h$  means:
\begin{align*}
\nabla{h}=\lambda_1\nabla{f}+\lambda_2\nabla{g}
\end{align*}
Since
\begin{align*}
\nabla{f}=<b+c,a+c,a+b>

\nabla{g}=<bc,ac,ab>

\nabla{h}=<1,1,1>
\end{align*}
we have that
\begin{align*}
\lambda_1(b+c)+\lambda_2bc=1 

\lambda_1(a+c)+\lambda_2ac=1

\lambda_1(a+b)+\lambda_2ab=1
\end{align*}
Subtracting pairwise equations yields:
\begin{align*}
(\lambda_2c+\lambda_1)(b-a)=0

(\lambda_2b+\lambda_1)(c-a)=0

(\lambda_2a+\lambda_1)(c-b)=0
\end{align*}
If  $a\neq{b}\neq{c}$ , then  $a=b=c=\frac{-\lambda_1}{\lambda_2}$  to satisfy the above equations, a contradiction. Therefore two variables must be equal.

