\iff\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{b}\right\rfloor=\frac{m^2-m}{b-1}.
\end{align*}
Clearly  $\tfrac{m^2-m}{b-1}=\tfrac{m^2}{b}-\epsilon$  (since  $\tfrac{m}{1}>\tfrac{m^2}{b}$ ), i.e., the condition will be satisfied so long as  $\tfrac{m^2-m}{b-1}$  is an integer. In other words, we need  $10$  values  $m\in[\sqrt{b},b^2)$  so that  $b-1$  divides  $m(m-1)$ . But since  $\gcd(m,m-1)=1$ , each prime power dividing  $b-1$  in turn divides exactly one of  $m$  and  $m-1$ . That is to say,  $m$  is equivalent to  $0$  or  $1$  modulo each said prime power, giving  $2^{p(b-1)}$  valid residues for  $m\pmod{b}$  (here  $p(b-1)$  is the number of primes dividing  $b-1$ ). Although there are potential edge cases to reduce the range from  $[0,b-1]$  (modulo  $b$  residues) to the desired  $[\sqrt{b},b^2)$ , certainly  $p(b-1)=3$  fails and  $p(b-1)=4$  works (as  $2^4\gg10$ ). The minimal  $b$  is therefore  $2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7+1=\boxed{211}$ .

