k&\equiv 1\pmod {2a}
k&\equiv -1\pmod {2^{v_2(2(b-1))}c}
\end{align*}
or vice versa (where the  $1$  and  $-1$  are swapped). We also have  $\gcd(a, c) = 1$  because  $\gcd(k+1, k-1) \le 2$ . When we combine these two congruences and solve for  $k$  we obtain  $k\equiv x\pmod {2(b-1)}$ , where  $x < 2b-2$ . Therefore, every possible pair of  $(2a, 2^{v_2(2(b-1))}c)$  contributes to a valid  $k$  (and thus a valid  $x$  and  $y$ ).

The number of pairs given a number  $b-1$  will simply be  $2^{\text{(number of prime factors of b - 1)}}$  (because there each odd prime can either go to  $a$  or  $c$ , and there are  $2$  ``places" for the singular power of  $2$  to go). Thus,  $b-1$  needs at least  $4$  distinct prime factors. Ergo,  $b = 2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdot 7 + 1 = \boxed{211}.$

