( $A_1 B_1$  and  $A_2 B_2$  both have length  $1$ ).

Since there are three solutions to the cubic, the other solution will correspond to a valid segment in  $\mathcal{F}$ . But because the only segment in  $\mathcal{F}$  to pass through  $C$  is  $AB$ , this solution must in fact be  $a=\frac{1}{2}$ . In other words, in our original quartic in  $a$ ,  $\frac{1}{2}$  must be a double root. Plugging this into our cubic yields
\begin{align*}
2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3-(4b-1)\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2+(8b^2-8b)\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)+4b^2&=0
\frac{1}{4}-\left(b-\frac{1}{4}\right)+4b^2-4b+4b^2&=0
8b^2-5b+\frac{1}{2}&=0
16b^2-10b+1&=0.
\end{align*}
Factoring gives  $$ (8b-1)(2b-1)=0. $$  Finally, since  $b$  cannot be  $\frac{1}{2}$  (if it was, it would be the same point as  $A$ ), we find that the only solution is  $\frac{1}{8}$ . This gives that  $C$  is the point  $$ \left(\frac{1}{8}, \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{8}\right), $$  so  $$ OC^2=\frac{1}{64}+\frac{27}{64}=\frac{7}{16}. $$  Our final answer is therefore  $$ 7+16=\boxed{023}. $$

