- Configurations which are not all the same color;
- Ordered pairs of non-boring words.
 In particular, there are  $(2^n-2)^2$  configurations in the first bullet.
*Proof.* The map is injective from the third condition in the problem statement. To check surjective: given two non-boring words as above, place a black chip at any cell whose row/column are both labeled  $B$ , and similarly for the white chips. Because neither word is boring, every row/column has at least one chip, and also there are no more chips that can be placed.  $\blacksquare$ On the other hand, there are exactly two configurations which are monochromatic (all the same color). Hence the final answer  $(2^n-2)^2 + 2$ .

