Thus,  $A$  lies on the radical axis of  $XY$ , as desired.

We will first show that \[\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(1+y)^2}\geq\frac{1}{1+xy}\] for all positive reals  $x,y$ . We have
\begin{align*}
    &\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(1+y)^2}\geq\frac{1}{1+xy}
    \impliedby&(1+xy)((1+x)^2+(1+y)^2)\geq (1+x)^2(1+y)^2
    \impliedby&2+2x+2y+x^2+y^2+2xy+2x^2y+2xy^2+x^3y+xy^3\geq 1+2x+2y+x^2+y^2+4xy+2x^2y+2xy^2+x^2y^2
    \impliedby&1+x^3y+xy^3\geq 2xy+x^2y^2
    \impliedby&1+2x^2y^2\geq 2xy+x^2y^2
    \impliedby&1+x^2y^2\geq 2xy
    \impliedby&2xy\geq 2xy
\end{align*}
which is true. We now also have
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2^i}\left(\frac{2}{1+x}\right)^{2^i}+\frac{1}{2^i}\left(\frac{2}{1+y}\right)^{2^i}
=&2^{2^i-i}\left(\left(\frac{1}{1+x}\right)^{2^i}+\left(\frac{1}{1+y}\right)^{2^i}\right)
\geq &2^{2^{i-1}-i+1}\left(\left(\frac{1}{1+x}\right)^2+\left(\frac{1}{1+y}\right)^2\right)^{2^{i-1}}
\geq &2^{2^{i-1}-i+1}\left(\frac{1}{1+xy}\right)^{2^{i-1}}
\geq &\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\left(\frac{2}{1+xy}\right)^{2^{i-1}}
\end{align*}

We now have
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_1}\right)^2+\dots+\frac{1}{2^n}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_n}\right)^{2^n}+\frac{1}{2^n}
=& \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_1}\right)^2+\dots+\frac{1}{2^n}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_n}\right)^{2^n}+\frac{1}{2^n}\left(\frac{2}{1+1}\right)^{2^n}
\geq&\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_1}\right)^2+\dots+\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_{n-1}}\right)^{2^{n-1}}+\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_n}\right)^{2^{n-1}}
\geq&\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_1}\right)^2+\dots+\frac{1}{2^{n-2}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_{n-2}}\right)^{2^{n-2}}+\frac{1}{2^{n-2}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_{n-1}a_n}\right)^{2^{n-2}}
\geq&\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_1}\right)^2+\dots+\frac{1}{2^{n-3}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_{n-3}}\right)^{2^{n-3}}+\frac{1}{2^{n-3}}\left(\frac{2}{1+a_{n-2}a_{n-1}a_n}\right)^{2^{n-3}}
\vdots&
\geq&\frac{2}{1+a_1a_2\dots a_n}
\end{align*}
from which the result follows.

By Lucas Theorem,  $\binom{t+i}{2a_i}$  is odd if and only if the digits where  $2a_i$  has a  $1$  in binary are a subset of those at which  $t+i$  has a  $1$ . Thus, we wish to show that there is a unique way to pair up elements between \[\{t,t+1,t+2,\dots 2t-1\}\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2t-2\}\] so that each element in the second set is achieved by deleting (i.e. replacing by  $0$ ) a nonzero amount of  $1$ s from the binary representation of an element in the first set.

We will show that there is a unique way to delete a single  $1$  from the binary representation of each element in the first set to get the elements of the second set. First note that since the odd elements must go to even numbers, we must delete the  $2^0$  digit of the odd numbers. Making the pairs  $(i,i-1)$  for each odd  $i$  in the first set, we are left with one of the two following possibilities for the remaining pairings depending on whether  $t$  is even or odd: \[\begin{cases} \{2s,2s+2,2s+4,\dots,4s-2\}\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2s-2\} & \text{ if }t=2s \{2s+2,2s+4,2s+6,\dots,4s\}\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2s-2\} & \text{ if }t=2s+1\end{cases}\] Ignoring the  $2^0$  digit, we have one of \[\begin{cases} \{s,s+1,s+2,\dots,2s-1\}\to\{0,1,2,\dots,s-1\} \{s+1,s+2,s+3,\dots,2s\}\to\{0,1,2,\dots,s-1\}\end{cases}\] Denote these cases by  $\mathcal A_s$  and  $\mathcal B_s$  respectively, for any  $s\geq 0$ .**Case 1:  $\mathcal A_s$  when  $s=2r$**
There are  $r$  even elements in the first set and  $r$  even elements in the second set. Since every even element must go to a even number, the even elements of the first set go to exactly the even elements of the second set, and the odd elements of the first set go to exactly the odd elements of the second set. This yields
\begin{align*}
\{2r,2r+2,2r+4,\dots,4r-2\}&\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2r-2\}
\{2r+1,2r+3,2r+5,\dots,4r-1\}&\to\{1,3,5,\dots,2r-1\}
\end{align*}
Ignoring the  $2^0$  digit, this reduces to
\begin{align*}
\{r,r+1,r+2,\dots,2r-1\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\{r,r+1,r+2,\dots,2r-1\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\end{align*}
which is  $\mathcal A_r$  and  $\mathcal A_r$ .**Case 2:  $\mathcal A_s$  when  $s=2r+1$**
There are  $r+1$  even elements in the first set and  $r$  even elements in the second set. Thus, one of the odd elements in the first set must go to an even number. However, this can only happen if we remove the  $2^0$  digit, or equivalently subtract  $1$ . Thus, that one odd element must be  $2r+1$ , going to  $2r$ . Now pairing up the even and odd elements as before, we have
\begin{align*}
\{2r+2,2r+4,2r+6,\dots,4r\}&\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2r-2\}
\{2r+3,2r+5,2r+7,\dots,4r+1\}&\to\{1,3,5,\dots,2r-1\}
\end{align*}
in addition to  $2r+1\to 2r$ . Ignoring the  $2^0$  digit, this reduces to
\begin{align*}
\{r+1,r+2,r+3,\dots,2r\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\{r+1,r+2,r+3,\dots,2r\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\end{align*}
which is  $\mathcal B_r$  and  $\mathcal B_r$ .**Case 3:  $\mathcal B_s$  when  $s=2r$**
There are  $r$  even elements in the first set and  $r$  even elements in the second set. Pairing up the even and odd elements, we have
\begin{align*}
\{2r+2,2r+4,2r+6,\dots,4r\}&\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2r-2\}
\{2r+1,2r+3,2r+5,\dots,4r-1\}&\to\{1,3,5,\dots,2r-1\}
\end{align*}
which reduces to
\begin{align*}
\{r+1,r+2,r+3,\dots,2r\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\{r,r+1,r+2,\dots,2r-1\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\end{align*}
which is  $\mathcal B_r$  and  $\mathcal A_r$ .**Case 4:  $\mathcal B_s$  when  $s=2r+1$**
There are  $r+1$  even elements and  $r$  odd elements in the second set. Pairing up the even and odd elements, we have
\begin{align*}
\{2r+2,2r+4,2r+6,\dots,4r+2\}&\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2r\}
\{2r+3,2r+5,2r+7,\dots,4r+1\}&\to\{1,3,5,\dots,2r-1\}
\end{align*}
which reduces to
\begin{align*}
\{r+1,r+2,r+3,\dots,2r+1\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r\}
\{r+1,r+2,r+3,\dots,2r\}&\to\{0,1,2,\dots,r-1\}
\end{align*}
which is  $\mathcal A_{r+1}$  and  $\mathcal B_r$ .

Now it is easy to check that  $\mathcal A_0,\mathcal B_0,\mathcal A_1,\mathcal B_1$  admit unique pairings. Then by induction,  $\mathcal A_s,\mathcal B_s$  admit unique pairings for  $s\geq 0$ . Then the original problem has a unique pairing.

We now have that there is a unique way to unique way to delete a single  $1$  from the binary representation of each element in the first set to get the elements of the second set in \[\{t,t+1,t+2,\dots 2t-1\}\to\{0,2,4,\dots,2t-2\}\] It follows that the total number of  $1$ s in the first set is exactly  $t$  more than the number of  $1$ s in the second set. Then in order to delete a nonzero number of  $1$ s from each of the elements in the first set to get the elements of the second set, we must delete only a single  $1$  from each element of the first set. But we have just shown that there is a unique way to do so, as desired.

