We claim that the answer is  $1$ ,  $2$ ,  $11$ ,  $22$ ,  $23$ , only. Note that  $n|2024$ , and  $n\le 45$ , so that  $n\in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 11, 22, 23, 44\}$ . **claim1.**  $n=1, 2, 11, 22, 23$  satisfys the condition.  $proof.$  Note that  $a\times b$  rectangle can be cut to  $n\times 1$  rectangle if  $n|a$ . Partition the grid to four of the  $22\times 23$  rectangle, as following. 
[asy]
draw((0, 0) -- (45, 0));
draw((45, 0) -- (45, 45));
draw((45, 45) -- (0, 45));
draw((0, 45) -- (0, 0));
draw((0, 23) -- (23, 23));
draw((22, 0) -- (22, 23));
draw((23, 45) -- (23, 22));
draw((22, 22) -- (45, 22));
fill((22, 22) -- (22, 23) -- (23, 23) -- (23, 22) -- cycle, black);
[/asy]
Since  $n|23$  or  $n|22$ , each of the rectangle can be cut to  $1\times n$ , or  $n\times 1$  rectangle, so we are done.**claim 2.** It is impossible to have  $n=4, 8, 44$ .  $proof.$  The idea is to color each of the square, either black or white. Moreover, let ( $n$ ,  $m$ ) denotes the square with the  $n$ th row,  $m$ th column. Then color the grid such that the square is black if and only if  $n$  is odd and  $m\equiv 1, 2\pmod 4$ , or  $n$  even and  $m\equiv 3, 0\pmod 4$ . The example with  $45$  replaced to  $5$  are shown below.[asy]
unitsize(1cm);
for(int i = 0 ; i <= 5 ; i = i + 1) draw((0, i) -- (5, i));
for(int i = 0 ; i <= 5 ; i = i + 1) draw((i, 0) -- (i, 5));
for(int i = 0 ; i <= 4 ; i = i + 2) fill((i, 5) -- (i + 1, 5) -- (i + 1, 3) -- (i, 3) -- cycle, black);
for(int i = 1 ; i <= 3 ; i = i + 2) fill((i, 3) -- (i + 1, 3) -- (i + 1, 1) -- (i, 1) -- cycle, black);
for(int i = 0 ; i <= 4 ; i = i + 2) fill((i, 0) -- (i + 1, 0) -- (i + 1, 1) -- (i, 1) -- cycle, black);
[/asy]
On the one hand, the number of black square, and white square must be equal, since each of the rectangle covers  $\frac{n}{2}$  black squares and same for white. but since the centre ( $23$ ,  $23$ ) is colored white, the number of the black square is \[
23\cdot 23 + 22\cdot 22 = 1013.
\]So the number of white square is  $2024-1013=1011\neq 1013$ , contradiction. 

And so, we are done!

