Inscribe the  $99$ -gon in a circle, because why not. Without loss of generality let the end configuration consist of the numbers sorted so that it increases in clockwise order. We claim that the answer is  $2401$ , with the worst starting position being the numbers sorted in counterclockwise order. This worst case part can be seen by tracking the numbers' movements relative to the diameter containing  $1$ : every number must end up on the opposite side of this diameter; moreover, if two numbers both cross this diameter for the final time on the same side, then they must swap with each other at some point. Thus, if  $a$  numbers cross on the  $1$ -side and  $b$  numbers cross on the opposite side, then there must be at least  $$ a + {a\choose 2} + {b\choose 2} = \frac{(a + 1)a}{2} + \frac{b(b-1)}{2} \ge 2401 $$  swaps by Karamata's inequality.  $\blacksquare$ Next, we show that all permutations can be rearranged to our desired end configuration in at most  $2401$  steps. We first prove stuff about permutations on a line.**Lemma 1:** Let  $\sigma$  be any permutation of  $(a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ ,  $\tau_1:= (a_1,\ldots, a_n)$ , and  $\tau_2:=(a_{\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor + 1},\ldots , a_n, a_1, \ldots a_{\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor})$ . If  $a$  and  $b$  are the minimum number of moves to turn  $\sigma$  into  $\tau_1$  and  $\tau_2$  respectively, then  $$ a + b \le 2\left({\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor\choose 2} + {\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil\choose 2}\right) + \left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\cdot \left\lceil\frac{n}{2}\right\rceil. $$  *Proof.* Suppose  $i < j$  and  $a_j$  comes before  $a_i$  in  $\sigma$ . If  $j \le \lfloor\tfrac{n}{2}\rfloor$  or  $i > \lfloor\tfrac{n}{2}\rfloor$  then  $a_i$  and  $a_j$  are inverted with respect to both  $\tau_1$  and  $\tau_2$ . Otherwise, if  $i \le \lfloor\tfrac{n}{2}\rfloor < j$ , then they are inverted with respect to  $\tau_1$  but in the correct order with respect to  $\tau_2$ . Thus, there are at most  $$ {\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor\choose 2} + {\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil\choose 2} $$  pairs which can be inverted with respect to both  $\tau_1$  and  $\tau_2$  and  $\left\lfloor\tfrac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\cdot \left\lceil\tfrac{n}{2}\right\rceil$  pairs which can only be inverted with respect to one of the two. To finish, just note that bubble sort completes in the same number of moves as number of inversions.  $\blacksquare$ **Lemma 2:** Consider the permutations  $(b_1, \ldots, b_k)$  and  $\sigma_2:= (b_{k+1}, \ldots, b_n)$  with  $(b_1, \ldots, b_n)$  a permutation of  $(a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ . If both  $\sigma_1$  and  $\sigma_2$  are sorted—that is, if both permutations are in the same order as  $(a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ —and if the minimum number of moves needed for  $(b_1, \ldots, b_n)$  and  $(b_{k+1}, \ldots, b_n, b_1, \ldots b_k)$  to be sorted into  $(a_1, \ldots, a_n)$  are  $a$  and  $b$  respectively, then  $$ a + b \le k\cdot (n - k) \le \left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor\cdot \left\lceil\frac{n}{2}\right\rceil. $$  *Proof.* Every pair is an inversion in exactly one of the two sequences. Moreover, every inversion involves exactly one element of  $\sigma_1$  and one element of  $\sigma_2$ . Bubble sort lowdiffs.  $\blacksquare$ Returning to the main problem, color the numbers from  $1$  to  $50$  red and  $51$  to  $99$  blue, then find an arc consisting of fifty numbers and exactly  $25$  of them being red: this must exist by pigeonhole principle and discrete continuity. Now, randomly sort the numbers of each half so that


- the monochromatic numbers of each half are in increasing clockwise order and
- the red and blue numbers on the circle become segregated, as in the diagram below.

