If  $a_i+a_j$  is fixed,  $G'(y)$  is a linear function of  $a_ia_j,$  therefore we can adjust  $(a_i,a_j)$  to  $(0,a_i+a_j)$  or  $(a_i+a_j/2,a_i+a_j/2).$  By a <details><summary>Common Fact</summary>有界闭集必有极限 in Chinese</details>, we can adjust  $(a_1,\ldots ,a_{n-1})$  to  $(u,u,\ldots ,u,0,\ldots ,0).$  Plug in we can easily get  $G'(y)\ge 0$  when  $y+a_{n-1,n}\ge 0.$  Therefore  $G(0)\ge G(-a_{n-1,n}),$  so WLOG  $a_{n-1,n}=0.$  Now let  $A_p:=\sum_{1\le i<j< n-1}a_{i,j}^p,$   $B_p:=\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}a_{i,n-1}^2,$   $C_p:=\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}a_{i,n}^2$  for  $p=2,4.$  We need to prove  $$ \left\lfloor\frac{(n+l-1)^2}4\right\rfloor (A_4+B_4+lC_4)\ge (A_2+B_2+lC_2)^2. $$  By induction hypothesis  $P(n-1,l+1),$   $$ \left\lfloor\frac{(n+l-1)^2}4\right\rfloor (A_4+(l+1)B_4)\ge (A_2+(l+1)B_2)^2, $$    $$ \left\lfloor\frac{(n+l-1)^2}4\right\rfloor (A_4+(l+1)C_4)\ge (A_2+(l+1)C_2)^2. $$  Therefore by Cauchy Schwartz Inequality
\begin{align*}(A_2+B_2+lC_2)^2&=\frac 1{(l+1)^2}\left((A_2+(l+1)B_2)+l(A_2+(l+1)C_2)\right)^2&\le \frac 1{l+1}\left((A_2+(l+1)B_2)^2+l(A_2+(l+1)C_2)^2\right)&\le\frac 1{l+1}\left\lfloor\frac{(n+l-1)^2}4\right\rfloor\left((A_4+(l+1)B_4)+l(A_4+(l+1)C_4)\right)&=\left\lfloor\frac{(n+l-1)^2}4\right\rfloor (A_4+B_4+lC_4).\Box\end{align*}

We claim  $\angle XEY=\frac12\angle BAC$ . Let  $\omega_1=(ABC)$  and  $\omega_2=(DEF)$  which are both tangent to  $AD$ . We claim the  $X$ -Apollonius circle of  $XAD$  passes through  $E$ . We have
\begin{align*}
\frac{EA}{ED}&=\frac{\sin\angle EDA}{\sin\angle EAD}
&=\frac{EDR_{\omega_1}}{EAR_{\omega_2}}
&=\sqrt{\frac{R_{\omega_1}}{R_{\omega_2}}}
\end{align*}

and
\begin{align*}
\frac{XA}{XD}&=\frac{\sin\angle XDA}{\sin\angle XAD}
&=\frac{DFR_{\omega_1}}{ABR_{\omega_2}}
&=\frac{XDR_{\omega_1}}{XAR_{\omega_2}}
&=\sqrt{\frac{R_{\omega_1}}{R_{\omega_2}}}.
\end{align*}

Similarly,  $\frac{YA}{YD}=\frac{XA}{XD}$ . Therefore,  $E$ ,  $X$ , and  $Y$  all lie on this circle. If the reflection of  $X$  over  $AD$  is  $X'$ , then  $X'$  also lies on this circle, and  $\angle YX'X=\frac12\angle A$ , so  $\angle XEY=\frac12\angle BAC$ , which is fixed.

<details><summary>another solution without trig</summary>$\frac{XA\cdot XB}{XD\cdot XF}=\frac{YA\cdot YC}{YD\cdot YF}$  so if  $\omega$  is the locus of  $Z$  such that  $\frac{\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_1}(Z)}{\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_2}(Z)}=\frac{XA\cdot XB}{XD\cdot XF}$ , then  $\omega$  passes through  $X$ ,  $Y$ , and  $E$ . Let the circumcircle of  $AXY$  intersects  $AD$  again at  $K$ , let the circumcircle of  $EXY$  pass through  $Z_1$  and  $Z_2$ , and let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $AD$ . Then,  $MA^2=MZ_1\cdot MZ_2$ , so  $(A,D;Z_1,Z_2)=-1$ . Therefore,  $DZ_1\cdot DZ_2=DX\cdot DY=DA\cdot DK$  implies  $K$  is the midpoint of  $Z_1Z_2$ . Since  $KX=KY$ ,  $K$  must be the circumcenter of  $EXY$ , so  $\angle XEY=\frac12\angle XKY=\frac12\angle XAY=\frac12\angle BAC$ , which is fixed.</details>

