The maximum value is  $\frac{4}{n^n(n+1)^{n+1}}$  which is achieved when  $a_{11}= \ldots = a_{n1} = \frac{2}{n(n+1)}$  and  $a_{1i} = \ldots = a_{ni}= \frac{1}{n(n+1)} \quad \forall 2 \leq i \leq n$ . 
Let  $k$  be a indice such that  $C_k = \max \{ C_i : 1 \leq i \leq n\}$ . Notice since  $a_{ij} \geq C_{j}$ , let  $b_{ij} = a_{ij}-C_{j} \geq 0$ . 
Notice that  $R_i \leq \max \{ C_j + b_{ij}\} \leq C_k + l_i$ , where  $l_i = \sum_{j=1}^{n}b_{ij}$ . Hence,   $$ \sum_{i=1}^{n}R_i \leq \sum_{i=1}^{n}C_k+l_i \leq n\cdot C_k+1-\sum_{1 \leq i, j \leq n}C_{j} = 1-n\sum_{\stackrel{1 \leq j \leq n}{j \neq k}}C_j $$  Also, notice that  $\sum_{j=1}^nC_j \leq \sum_{j=1}^n\frac{a_{1j} + \ldots + a_{nj}}{n} = \frac{1}{n}$ , so  $C_k \leq \frac{1}{n}-\sum_{\stackrel{1 \leq j \leq n}{j \neq k}}C_j$ . 
Let  $r = \frac{1}{n}-\sum_{\stackrel{1 \leq j \leq n}{j \neq k}}C_j$ . Hence \begin{align*}
    C_1\ldots C_n(R_1+\ldots+R_n) &\leq 
    C_1 \ldots C_n \cdot \frac{r}{C_k}\cdot nr  &= nr^2\prod_{\stackrel{1 \leq i \leq n}{i \neq k}}C_i  & \leq \frac{n}{2^{n-1}}\cdot (\frac{r+r+\sum_{\stackrel{1 \leq i \leq n}{i \neq k}}2C_i}{n+1})^{n+1}  & = \frac{n}{2^{n-1}}(\frac{\frac2 n}{n+1})^{n+1} = \frac{4}{n^n(n+1)^{n+1}}
\end{align*}
as desired.

