Writing everything in terms of  $\overrightarrow{OA}, \overrightarrow{OB}, \overrightarrow{OC},$  and  $\overrightarrow{OD}$  and rearranging, we get   $$ 2(\overrightarrow{OB}+\overrightarrow{OD})=3(\overrightarrow{OA}+\overrightarrow{OC}). $$   Now, note that  $\overrightarrow{OB}$  and  $\overrightarrow{OD}$  have opposite directions and  $\overrightarrow{OA}$  and  $\overrightarrow{OC}$  have opposite directions. Since these two directions are not the same, it follows that both sides must be the  $0$  vector, i.e.   $$ \overrightarrow{OB}+\overrightarrow{OD}=\overrightarrow{OA}+\overrightarrow{OC}=0. $$    Hence, we get
\begin{align*}
\overrightarrow{AB}-\overrightarrow{DC} &= \overrightarrow{OA}-\overrightarrow{OB}+\overrightarrow{OC}-\overrightarrow{OD} 
&= (\overrightarrow{OA}+\overrightarrow{OC})-(\overrightarrow{OB}+\overrightarrow{OD}) 
&= 0-0 
&= 0,
\end{align*}
implying that  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{DC}$  are parallel. Analogously, we can show that  $\overline{AD}$  and  $\overline{BC}$  are parallel, so it results that  $ABCD$  is a parallelogram, as desired.  $\square$

Let's complex bash this one. If we let  $\triangle ABC$  be inscribed in the unit circle we have  $$ |a|=|b|=|c|=1 $$    $$ h=a+b+c $$    $$ x=\frac{b+c}2 $$    $$ o = 0 $$    $$ o' = b+c $$  Now we find the coordinate of  $Y$ . Since  $Y$  lies on  $AB$  we have  $$ \overline{y} = \frac{a+b-y}{ab} $$  Since  $HY\perp HX$ , we have  $$ \frac{h-y}{h-x} \in i\mathbb{R} $$    $$ \frac{2(a+b+c-y)}{2a+b+c} = -\frac{2(ab+cy)}{ab+ac+2bc} $$    $$ (a+b+c-y)(ab+ac+2bc) = -(ab+cy)(2a+b+c) $$  Then
\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{(a+b+c)(ab+ac+2bc) + ab(2a+b+c)}{(ab+ac+2bc) - c(2a+b+c)} 
&= \frac{3a^2b+a^2c+2ab^2+5abc+ac^2+2b^2c+2bc^2}{(a+c)(b-c)} 
&= \frac{3ab+ac+2b^2+2bc}{b-c}
\end{align*}
Similarly,  $$ z = \frac{3ac+ab+2c^2+2bc}{c-b} $$  Then  $$ y-h = \frac{(b+c)(2a+b+c)}{b-c} $$  and  $$ z-h = \frac{(b+c)(2a+b+c)}{c-b} $$  so  $HY = HZ$ . This establishes part (a).

For part (b), we quickly find  $$ y-a = \frac{2(a+b)(b+c)}{b-c} $$    $$ z-a = \frac{2(a+c)(b+c)}{c-b} $$  Then  $$ (y-a) + (z-a) = \frac{2(a+b)(b+c) - 2(a+c)(b+c)}{b-c} = 2(b+c) = 2(o'-o) $$  and this finishes part (b).  $\blacksquare$

