*Proof.* Imagine walking from cell  $1$  to cell  $n^2$  along the original  $1\times n^2$  strip, and tracing the path  $P$  through the final location of those cells (in order) in the  $n\times n$  square. Note that  $P$  hits every cell in the square exactly once.

The key observation is that whenever  $P$  jumps from a piece  $X$  in row  $i$  to a piece  $Y$  row  $j$ , the right edge of  $X$  must have the same color as the left edge of  $Y$ . By construction, this implies that  $i$  and  $j$  are connected in  $G$ . 

