\[ |E(G)| = \sum_{i=1}^n (|R_i| - 1) \ge n-1, \]
which by the first claim means that there are at least  $n-1$  inner edges. Thus, Paul must have used at least  $2n-1$  pieces.**Remark:** [Motivation] In the problem statement, the cells of the  $1\times n^2$  strip are labeled with the numbers  $1, \dots, n^2$  (although of course, only their residues modulo  $n$  matter). When thinking about how the strip rearranges into the square, I think it's natural to consider the path  $P$  formed by cell  $1$ , cell  $2$ , ..., cell  $n^2$  in the final  $n\times n$  square. Then one can ask: what kinds of jumps can happen? At this point, I noticed that  $P$  can only jump to a new row using edges of the same color, and that any edge with color  $C_i$  in a row  $j\neq i$  must be an inner edge. Thus jumping between rows forces new inner edges, leading to the solution above.

