<details><summary>Solution</summary>We claim the answer is  $D=50$ , which is the best we can do if   $$ a_1= \dots =a_{50} = 0.5\text{ and }a_{51}= \dots = a_{100}=1.5 $$  Let  $m=\{a_1\}+\dots+\{a_{100}\}$ . We set  $b_i$  to be  $\lceil a_i\rceil$  for  $m$  randomly-chosen values of  $i$  and to be  $\lfloor a_i\rfloor$  for the rest. Clearly,  $b_1+\dots+b_{100}=a_1+\dots+a_{100}$  Thus,  $$ |b_i-a_i| = \begin{cases}
1-\{a_i\} &\text{ with probability } \frac{m}{100} 
\{a_i\} & \text{ with probability } 1-\frac{m}{100}.
\end{cases} $$  Therefore, we have that
\begin{align*}
\mathbb E\left[\sum_{i=1}^{100} |b_i-a_i|\right]
&= \frac{m}{100} \sum_{i=1}^{100} \big(1-\{a_i\}\big)
+ \frac{(100-m)}{100}\sum_{i=1}^{100} \{a_i\} 
&= \frac{m(100-m)}{50} \leq 50,
\end{align*}
so this process works with positive probability.</details>

