 The answer is  $\boxed{-\tfrac{1}{4}}$ .
By properties of logarithms we have, 
\[S_n = \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{k=1}^n\log{k^k}-\log\left(\sqrt{n}\right).\]
The key idea is to somehow convert this sum into a mesh sum i.e, we will want the sum to be in terms of  $k/n$ , so we subtract and add  $\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{k}{n^2}\log n=\log n^{1+\tfrac{1}{2n}}$  to the sum to get,
\begin{align*}
	\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\frac{k}{n}\log k-\frac{k}{n}\log n\right)+\frac{\log n}{2n} &=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{k}{n}\log\left(\frac{k}{n}\right) +\frac{\log n}{2n}
																										  &\to \int_{0}^1 x\log x\mathrm{d}x
																								  &= -\frac{1}{4}.
\end{align*}
Which is as desired.  $\square$

