Take any prime divisor  $p$  of  $ab+1$  and let  $t=\nu_p(ab+1)$ . Then
\begin{align*}
  a^n+b &\equiv a^n - \frac 1a \equiv \frac{a^{n+1}-1}{a} \pmod{p^t}
  b^n+a &\equiv b^n - \frac 1b \equiv \frac{b^{n+1}-1}{b} \pmod{p^t}
\end{align*}
So  $p^t\mid \gcd(a^n+b,b^n+a)$  if and only if  $\operatorname{lcm}(x,y) \mid n+1$ , where  $x$ ,  $y$  are the orders of  $a$ ,  $b$  mod  $p^t$ , respectively. If  $p^t>2$  and  $(a,b)\neq (1,1)$ , then  $\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)>1$ , so the sequence is not eventually constant.
Thus  $p^t=2$ , implying  $ab+1=2$ , which still gives  $(a,b)=(1,1)$ .

