I claim that the only such pair is  $(1,1).$  It is easy to see that this pair works because for all positive integers  $n,$  we have
\begin{align*}
    \gcd(1^n+1,1^n+1) &= \gcd(1+1, 1+1) 
    &= \gcd(2,2) 
    &= 2.
\end{align*}
Now, assume that at least one of  $a$  or  $b$  is greater than  $1.$  Suppose for the sake of contradiction that there exists some positive integers  $g$  and  $N$  such that   $$ \gcd(a^n+b, b^n+a)=g \ \forall \ n \ge N. $$   Then, let us choose a positive integer  $k$  such that   $$ k \ge \left\lceil \frac{N+1}{\varphi(ab+1)}\right\rceil. $$   It follows that we must have   $$ \gcd(a^{k\varphi(ab+1)-1}+b, b^{k\varphi(ab+1)-1}+a)=g. $$   Clearly, we have  $\gcd(a,ab+1)=1,$  so Euler's Totient Theorem gives us
\begin{align*}
    a^{k\varphi(ab+1)-1}+b &\equiv a^{-1}+b 
    &\equiv \frac{1}{a}+b 
    &\equiv \frac{ab+1}{a} 
    &\equiv 0 \pmod{ab+1}.
\end{align*}
Analogously, we have   $$ b^{k\varphi(ab+1)-1}+a, $$   so it results that   $$ ab+1 \mid g. $$   Therefore, we have   $$ ab+1 \mid \gcd(a^{k\varphi(ab+1)}+b, b^{k\varphi(ab+1)}+a). $$   By Euler's Totient Theorem once again, we have
\begin{align*}
    a^{k\varphi(ab+1)}+b &\equiv b+1 \pmod{ab+1}.
\end{align*}
Hence, it follows that we must have   $$ b+1 \equiv 0 \pmod{ab+1}. $$   However, since at least one of  $a$  or  $b$  is greater than  $1,$  we have   $$ 0<b+1<ab+1, $$   contradiction. Thus, our original supposition that there exist positive integers  $a$  and  $b$  for which at least one of them is greater than  $1$  and there exist positive integers  $g$  and  $N$  such that   $$ \gcd(a^n+b, b^n+a)=g \ \forall \ n \ge N $$   must have been false, concluding the proof.  $\square$

