Let  $E$  and  $F$  be the tangency point of  $\omega$  with  $AC$  and  $AB$  respectively. Let  $G$  be the reflection of  $E$  over  $K$ . Let  $S$  and  $T$  be the antipodes of  $E$  and  $F$  in  $\omega$  respectively. Now let  $N = BG\cap AI$  and  $O = AI\cap BC$ .

It is well known that  $\overline{B-S-G}$  are collinear. Now due to a homothety centered at  $E$  with factor  $\frac{1}{2}$ , we get that  $IK \parallel SG$ . This means that  $\angle KIA = \angle GNA = \angle BNP$ .

Now we show that  $NY \parallel AC$ . To show this, we prove that  $\frac{ON}{NA}=\frac{OY}{YC}$ .

Firstly,
\[
\frac{CG}{GA}=\frac{CB}{BA}\cdot \frac{\sin(\angle CBG)}{\sin(\angle GBA)}\implies \frac{\sin(\angle CBG)}{\sin(\angle GBA)}=\frac{CG}{GA}\cdot \frac{AB}{BC}=\frac{AE}{EC}\cdot \frac{c}{a}=\frac{s-a}{s-c}\cdot \frac{c}{a}
.\] 

We also have  $OB = \frac{ac}{c+b}$ . Thus,
\[
\frac{ON}{NA}=\frac{OB}{BA}\cdot \frac{\sin(\angle OBN)}{\sin(\angle NBA)}=\frac{\frac{ac}{c+b}}{c}\cdot \frac{\sin(\angle CBG)}{\sin(\angle GBA)}=\frac{a}{c+b}\cdot \frac{s-a}{s-c}\cdot \frac{c}{a} = \frac{(s-a)c}{(b+c)(s-c)}
.\] 

Now we calculate  $\frac{OY}{YC}$  similarly.

Before we start, let  $h_{c}$  denote the height of the  $C$ -altitude and  $r$  be the inradius. We can derive that  $h_{c}=\frac{2\Delta}{c}$  and  $r = \frac{\Delta}{s}$ . Firstly,
\[
\frac{CY}{CB}=\frac{\operatorname{dist}(C,YT)}{\operatorname{dist}(C,AB)}=\frac{h_{c}-2r}{h_{c}}=1 - 2 \frac{r}{h_{c}}=1- 2 \frac{\frac{\Delta}{s}}{\frac{2\Delta}{c}}=1 - \frac{c}{s}=\frac{s-c}{s}
.\] 
This gives us that  $CY = \frac{s-c}{s}\cdot CB = \frac{a(s-c)}{s}$ .

We also have that  $OC = \frac{ab}{b+c}$ . Thus,

\begin{align*}
  \frac{OY}{YC} = \frac{OC-YC}{YC} &= \frac{OC}{YC}-1
                                   &= \frac{\frac{ab}{b+c}}{\frac{a(s-c)}{s}}-1
                                   &= \frac{bs}{(b+c)(s-c)}-1
                                   &= \frac{bs - (b+c)(s-c)}{(b+c)(s-c)}
                                   &= \frac{bs - bs + bc - cs + c^2}{(b+c)(s-c)}
                                   &= \frac{c(b+c-s)}{(b+c)(s-c)}
                                   &= \frac{c(s-a)}{(b+c)(s-c)}
                                   &= \frac{ON}{NA}
.\end{align*}

This gives us that  $NY \parallel AC$ . Now note that  $\angle PBC = \angle  PAC = \angle PNY$  which gives us that  $BNYP$  is cyclic.

Thus,
\[
  \angle KIA = \angle GNA = \angle BNP = \angle BYP = \angle XYP.
\]
Similarly, we also get that  $\angle YXP = \angle LIA$ .

So,
\[
  \angle KIL = \angle KIA + \angle LIA = \angle XYP + \angle YXP = 180^{\circ} - \angle YPX \implies \angle KIL + YPX = 180^{\circ}
\]
and we are done!

