We claim the smallest  $c$  is  $2$ .

First, we show  $c\geq 2$ .
Let  $f(x)=x-2\{x\}=2\lfloor x\rfloor-x$ .
If  $r$  is an integer,  $f(r)+f(-r)=r-r=0$ . Otherwise,  $f(r)+f(-r)=r-2\{r\}-r-2\{-r\}=-2(\{r\}+\{-r\})=-2$ .
We show this  $f$  works.

The first equation is
\begin{align*}
2\lfloor x+f(y)\rfloor-x-f(y)&=2\lfloor x\rfloor-x+y
2\lfloor x+2\lfloor y\rfloor-y\rfloor&=2\lfloor x\rfloor+2\lfloor y\rfloor
2\lfloor x-y\rfloor+2\lfloor y\rfloor&=2\lfloor x\rfloor
\lfloor x-y\rfloor+\lfloor y\rfloor&=\lfloor x\rfloor
\{x-y\}+\{y\}&=\{x\}.
\end{align*}
Similarly, the second is  $\{y-x\}+\{x\}=\{y\}$ . Therefore, if  $\{x\}\geq\{y\}$ , the first equation works since  $\{x-y\}=\{x\}-\{y\}$ , and otherwise, the second works as  $\{y\}\geq\{x\}$  implies  $\{y-x\}=\{y\}-\{x\}$ . This  $f$  has  $2$  distinct rationals of the form  $f(r)+f(-r)$ :  $0$  and  $-2$ .

Now, we show  $c\leq 2$ .

If  $f(a)=0$ , then  $x=y=a$  gives  $f(a+f(a))=a+f(a)$ , so  $a=0$ .
Let  $y=f(0)$  and  $x=-f(f(0))$ . Then, either  $$ f(0)=f(-f(f(0)))+f(0)\quad\text{or}\quad f(f(-f(f(0))+f(0)))=0. $$  Either way,  $f(a)=0$  for some  $a$ , so we must have  $f(0)=0$ .
Now,  $y=-f(x)$  gives  $$ f(x+f(-f(x)))=0\quad\text{or}\quad f(0)=x+f(-f(x)), $$   so  $x+f(-f(x))=0$ . Then,  $y=-f(k)$  gives  $$ f(x+f(-f(k)))=f(x)-f(k)\quad\text{or}\quad f(f(x)-f(k))=x+f(-f(k)), $$   so since  $f(-f(k))=-k$ , we get  $$ f(x-k)=f(x)-f(k)\quad\text{or}\quad f(f(x)-f(k))=x-k. $$  In the second case,   $$ f(k-x)=f(-f(f(x)-f(k)))=f(k)-f(x). $$  Therefore,  $f(x-k)+f(k)=f(x)$  or  $f(k-x)+f(x)=f(k)$ .
