&= b+r_2+a-r_2 = y+f(x).
\end{align*}
If  $r_1 \leq r_2$ , then the reverse relation holds. Also,  $f(x) + f(-x) = 0$  when  $x$  is an integer and  $-2$  when  $x$  is a non-integer, so it satisfies  $c=2$ .**<span style="color:purple">Remark:**</span> While the construction seems out of the blue, it's actually very well motivated. We can consider the set  $S$  of fixed points of  $f$ , and it is not hard to prove that  $S$  is a vector space over  $\mathbb Z$ . So naturally we would expect  $S = \mathbb Z$ , which leads to an  $f$  that behaves ``differently" based on whether  $x$  is an integer or not. Also, recall that  $-f$  must be an involution, and  $x + f(x)$  is a fixed point (thus an integer). This basically narrows down only one possibility of  $f$ , which works.

