There is a strategy for the snail that requires at most 2 restarts/3 attempts.


- Step 1: Exhaustively find the monster in  $\textbf{row 2}$  this forces the first restart.
- <span style="color:green">Case 1-</span> The monster  $\textbf{in row 2 is not in column 1 or 2023}.$  In this case, exhaustively find the monster in row 3, forcing the second restart. Say the monster in row 2 is at position  $ x, $  and the monster in row 3 is at position  $y.$    $\text{Without loss of generality  by symmetry}, \,x < y. $   The path  $(1,x-1), (2,x-1),(3,x-1),(3,x),(4,x)\hdots,(2024,x)$  is guaranteed to have no monsters, since there is at most one monster per column.
- <span style="color:green">Case 2-</span> The monster in  $\textbf{row 2 is in column 1. }$  The case where the monster in row  $1$  is at column  $2023$  is symmetric.
**<span style="color:red">Claim-</span>**  For any board with n monsters,  $n+1$  columns, and n+2 rows, with  $n \geq 2, $  no monsters in  $\text{row 1}\, \text{or}\, n+2, $  and one known monster at position  $(2,1), $  then we can travel from  $\textbf{row 1}$  to row  $n+2$  with at most one more restart.

<u>Proof by induction on  $n$</u>**<span style="color:#red">Case  $n=2,$ </span>** we are looking at a board with  $\textbf{ 2 monsters, 3 columns, 4 rows,}$   and one of the two monsters known to be at  $(2,1).$  Certainly we can make it to  $\text{row 4}$   with at most  $1$  more restart as there is at most one more unknown monster. If we discover that monster  $,$  we can take the remaining column that does not contain a monster.

Since we know there is a monster at  $(2,1),$  will explore  $(1,3),(2,3) (3,3)\hdots(3,n+1).$  If we  hit a monster from  $(3,3)\hdots(3,n+1)$  second restart, then the monster in  $\textbf{row 3 is not in column 2, and the path }\,(1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(3,1)\hdots(n+2,1) $  is guaranteed to have no monster because there is at most one monster per column, and can take this path on our third attempt.

If we do not hit a monster from  $(3,3)\hdots(3,n+1),\,\textbf{ then  the monster in row 3 must be at column 2, since it can't be in column 1 as there is a monster at} \,(2,1)$  
Note that finding this information did not require us to restart. We can then shrink the game board, looking only at columns  $[2:n+1]$  and rows  $[ 2:n+2]. $  We know there are exactly  $n-1$   monsters in this range, with n columns,  $\textbf{n+1  rows, a known monster at adjusted index (2,1),  and  no monsters in adjusted rows 1 or n+1}. $  
