Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $AB$ ,  $T=EF\cap \odot(ABC)$ . Also let  $P = \odot(ECF)\cap BC\; (\neq C)$ .

Now temporarily let  $T' = CO\cap \odot(ABC)$ .

Now firstly, note that as  $\measuredangle ECB = 90^\circ$ . This means that  $E$  and  $B$  are antipodes and thus  $\overline{E-O-B}$  are collinear. Now further note that as  $ET \parallel BC$ , we get that  $\measuredangle TEC = \measuredangle ECB = 90^\circ$ . This also means that  $C$  and  $T$  are antipodes and so,  $\overline{C-O-T}$  are also collinear.

Now note that,
\[ \measuredangle FEO = \measuredangle TEB = \measuredangle TCB = \measuredangle ACT = \measuredangle FCO \]
from which we get that  $CEFO$  is cyclic.

Now by Reim's Theorem on  $\left\{\odot(ECF), \odot(ABC)\right\}$  with lines  $EF$  and  $CO$ , we get that  $TT \parallel OF$ . But clearly  $TT\parallel AB\implies OF \parallel AB$ . Now as  $OT\perp AB$ , we also get that  $OT \perp OF$ .

This means that  $\measuredangle CEF=\measuredangle COF=\measuredangle CPF= 90^\circ$  which gives  $CEFOP$  are cyclic.

Also note that  $\measuredangle CEG = \measuredangle CMG = 90^\circ$  which gives that  $CMGE$  is cyclic.

Now note that  $\measuredangle POE = 90^\circ$  and that  $O$  is the midpoint of  $EB$ . Using these, we conclude that  $PO$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $EB$ .

Now to finish, we have,
\begin{align*}
    \measuredangle EPB &= \measuredangle EPO + \measuredangle OPB
    &= 2 \measuredangle EPO
    &= 2 \measuredangle ECO
    &= 2 \measuredangle ECM
    &= 2 \measuredangle EGM
    &= 2 \measuredangle EGB
.\end{align*}

This means that  $P$  lies on the circle containing the center of  $\odot(EGB)$ . Now along with the information that  $P$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $EB$ , we conclude that  $P$  is indeed the center of  $\odot(EGB)$  and we are done. :yoda:**Remark:** I forgot to write down the proof for  $CEFO$  cyclic during the exam although I had headsolved it while solving it in the rough  :wallbash_red: . I got 7/17 due to this reason and didn't make IMOTC. T__T

