Let  $P$  be a finite set of primes and let  $D_P$  be the set of values such that they are only divisible by primes in  $P$ . 

<span style="color:#A64D79"><u>*Claim:*</u></span> We can find infinitely many primes  $q$  such that no value  $d\in D_P$  has the property that  $d\equiv p-1\pmod q$ .**Proof:**
Let the primes in  $P$  be  $p_1$ ,  $p_2$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $p_k$  excluding  $2$ . If  $p_i\equiv 3\pmod 4$ , let  $r_i$  be a quadratic non-residue modulo  $p_i$ , if  $p_i\equiv 1 \pmod 4$ , let  $r_i$  be a quadratic residue modulo  $p_i$ . Thus let  $q$  be any prime such that:
\begin{align}
q \equiv 
\begin{cases}
7\pmod 8 
r_1 \pmod{p_1} 
r_2 \pmod{p_2} 
\vdots 
r_{k-1} \pmod{p_{k-1}}
r_k \pmod{p_k} 
\end{cases}
\end{align}
By dirchlet's theorem there are infinitely many such primes. We know that  $2$  is a quadratic residue  $\pmod q$ , and that all primes  $p_1$ ,  $p_2$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $p_k$  are quadratic residues  $\pmod q$ , however  $p-1$  is not a quadratic residue  $\pmod q$ , thus for any  $d \in D_P$ , we know that  $d\not\equiv p-1 \pmod q$  for all  $d$ .  $\square$ Thus if the set of primes dividing  ${b_n}$  is finite there exists infinitely many primes which do not divide  ${a_n}$  which is a contradiction.

