Notice that we can 'snake' the  $n^2+2n$  red dominoes resulting in  $k=n^2+2n$  moves.
--------------------------------------------------------**Bound:**
First observe that the coordinates of the vacant square are invariant  $\pmod{3}$ .

Now we claim that a square can never be vacant twice. Assume their was a cycle and consider the smallest such one, with the vacant square moving from cells  $c_1,c_2,\dots,c_n$  back to  $c_1$ . Notice that  $n>2$  by the restraints of the problem and we must have  $c_i$  and  $c_{i+1}$  being  $3$  away either vertically or horizontally ( $c_{n+1}=c_1$ ). Thus in this cycle we must have some number of triominoes and  $1$  vacant square, but the cycle consists of exactly  $3n$  cells, a contradiction.

Thus the vacant square can visit once at most  $(n+1)^2$  cells so it may not make more than  $(n+1)^2-1=n^2+2n$  moves.

