Proof that this is always possible by induction, 
If  $n=0$ , it's clear that  $a_0=5000$  satisfies the inequality. 
If  $n=k$  satisfies the inequality, we claim it's possible for Penny to force  $a_{k+2}$  to satisfy the inequality for all even nonnegative integer  $k\leq 138$ . 
We can see that  $1\leq a_k-a_{k+1}\leq k+1$  and  $1\leq a_{k+2}-a_{k+1}\leq k+2$ . Then, 
\begin{align*}
\frac{(140-k)(142+k)}{4}&\leq a_k\leq\frac{(140-k)(144+k)}{4} 
-\frac{k^2}{4}-\frac{k}{2}+4970&\leq a_k\leq-\frac{k^2}{4}-k+5040 
\end{align*}

Then, if  $a_k=-\frac{k^2}{4}-k+5040$ , Penny can force  $a_k-a_{k+2}=k+3$ , else Penny can force  $a_k-a_{k+2}=k+2$ . 
